1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,749
Sunday night.

2
00:00:01,749 --> 00:00:04,071
Forty-eight answer sheets from this morning's mock

3
00:00:04,071 --> 00:00:05,023
test.

4
00:00:05,023 --> 00:00:06,370
One red pen.

5
00:00:06,370 --> 00:00:08,980
And a cup of chai that went cold an hour ago.

6
00:00:09,130 --> 00:00:10,663
You know how this goes.

7
00:00:10,663 --> 00:00:12,253
Tick, cross, tick…

8
00:00:12,253 --> 00:00:13,066
total.

9
00:00:13,066 --> 00:00:15,783
Every mark into the register, then the rank list.

10
00:00:15,783 --> 00:00:18,006
And somewhere around Tuesday, it's finally done.

11
00:00:18,156 --> 00:00:21,000
But the one question that really matters —

12
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,380
which questions did my batch get wrong,

13
00:00:23,380 --> 00:00:24,106
and why?

14
00:00:24,106 --> 00:00:25,633
never gets answered.

15
00:00:25,633 --> 00:00:28,094
To know that, you'd have to count nine hundred and

16
00:00:28,094 --> 00:00:29,975
sixty answers by hand.

17
00:00:29,975 --> 00:00:31,681
Nobody has that kind of Sunday.

18
00:00:31,831 --> 00:00:34,931
On TestoZa, the checking is done the second they press

19
00:00:34,931 --> 00:00:35,616
Submit.

20
00:00:35,616 --> 00:00:38,472
But then you open the results, and it's a screen full

21
00:00:38,472 --> 00:00:39,575
of numbers.

22
00:00:39,575 --> 00:00:41,630
So today, let's read them together.

23
00:00:41,630 --> 00:00:44,440
Both result pages, every number, and exactly what

24
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:46,392
to do with them on Monday morning.

25
00:00:47,542 --> 00:00:50,433
First, a promise, because I know this is the worry.

26
00:00:50,433 --> 00:00:52,047
Your marks register?

27
00:00:52,047 --> 00:00:53,672
That's the Excel file.

28
00:00:53,672 --> 00:00:55,820
The rank list for the notice board?

29
00:00:55,820 --> 00:00:58,444
One tap, and it's on WhatsApp.

30
00:00:58,444 --> 00:01:01,207
Opening one student's copy to see where they went

31
00:01:01,207 --> 00:01:01,834
wrong?

32
00:01:01,834 --> 00:01:03,866
You can open every single answer sheet.

33
00:01:03,866 --> 00:01:05,724
And your discussion class stays.

34
00:01:05,724 --> 00:01:07,925
It just gets shorter, and a lot sharper.

35
00:01:08,075 --> 00:01:10,896
And then there are a few things paper simply can't

36
00:01:10,896 --> 00:01:12,011
tell you.

37
00:01:12,011 --> 00:01:13,063
Let's go find them.

38
00:01:14,343 --> 00:01:16,363
Your results live in two places.

39
00:01:16,363 --> 00:01:19,672
On My Tests, every exam you've run has a Results button.

40
00:01:19,672 --> 00:01:23,155
And in the sidebar, All Submissions keeps every exam

41
00:01:23,155 --> 00:01:25,314
your candidates have ever written.

42
00:01:25,314 --> 00:01:27,015
Let's start with the quick view.

43
00:01:27,115 --> 00:01:28,845
Click Results.

44
00:01:28,845 --> 00:01:32,630
Up top: the exam, its status — this one has ended

45
00:01:32,630 --> 00:01:35,859
twenty questions, forty marks, twenty minutes.

46
00:01:35,959 --> 00:01:39,198
These four boxes are your attendance register.

47
00:01:39,198 --> 00:01:41,044
Fifty-two candidates started.

48
00:01:41,044 --> 00:01:43,273
Writing now shows who's still writing,

49
00:01:43,273 --> 00:01:45,096
live, during the exam.

50
00:01:45,096 --> 00:01:47,372
It's over, so, zero.

51
00:01:47,372 --> 00:01:49,020
Forty-eight submitted.

52
00:01:49,020 --> 00:01:52,039
And four dropped: they started, but never handed in.

53
00:01:52,139 --> 00:01:55,448
Tap Dropped, and you can see exactly when each one

54
00:01:55,448 --> 00:01:58,025
left, and how much they'd answered.

55
00:01:58,025 --> 00:02:01,125
Akash answered two questions and went quiet at three

56
00:02:01,125 --> 00:02:02,414
minutes past ten.

57
00:02:02,414 --> 00:02:04,376
Sonal answered fourteen.

58
00:02:04,376 --> 00:02:05,839
That's a different story.

59
00:02:05,839 --> 00:02:08,033
One quick call tells you if it was the network,

60
00:02:08,033 --> 00:02:09,845
the phone, or something else.

61
00:02:09,845 --> 00:02:12,596
On paper, both of them were just…

62
00:02:12,596 --> 00:02:13,285
absent.

63
00:02:13,385 --> 00:02:15,695
Now Submitted, everyone who finished.

64
00:02:15,695 --> 00:02:18,633
Average, highest and lowest, right at the top.

65
00:02:18,633 --> 00:02:21,175
Then every score, with the percentage under it:

66
00:02:21,175 --> 00:02:24,298
blue means forty percent or more, red means below.

67
00:02:24,298 --> 00:02:26,156
A tick for right, a cross for wrong,

68
00:02:26,156 --> 00:02:27,758
a dash for left blank.

69
00:02:27,758 --> 00:02:30,006
Time taken, and when they submitted.

70
00:02:30,106 --> 00:02:31,093
Want ranks?

71
00:02:31,093 --> 00:02:32,428
Switch on Rank.

72
00:02:32,428 --> 00:02:35,470
Hover the little i for the tie-breakers.

73
00:02:35,470 --> 00:02:37,931
Same as the real exam: higher marks first,

74
00:02:37,931 --> 00:02:40,079
then fewer wrong answers, then fewer left blank,

75
00:02:40,079 --> 00:02:41,971
then whoever submitted first.

76
00:02:41,971 --> 00:02:44,270
Look at Sneha and Rahul: both thirty-three,

77
00:02:44,270 --> 00:02:46,638
both two wrong, both one blank.

78
00:02:46,638 --> 00:02:48,937
Sneha submitted four minutes earlier,

79
00:02:48,937 --> 00:02:50,318
so Sneha is second.

80
00:02:50,418 --> 00:02:52,752
This Submitted heading is a filter too.

81
00:02:52,752 --> 00:02:54,923
If one test was written on two days,

82
00:02:54,923 --> 00:02:57,198
pick a day, and the ranks follow.

83
00:02:57,198 --> 00:02:59,776
Excel downloads the whole list: names,

84
00:02:59,776 --> 00:03:01,831
roll numbers, marks, ranks.

85
00:03:01,831 --> 00:03:02,747
That's your register.

86
00:03:02,747 --> 00:03:03,456
Done.

87
00:03:03,456 --> 00:03:05,200
And search finds anyone in a second.

88
00:03:05,586 --> 00:03:08,802
Now click this, on any row, and you get that candidate's

89
00:03:08,802 --> 00:03:10,393
whole answer sheet.

90
00:03:10,393 --> 00:03:12,749
Jyoti: eighteen out of forty.

91
00:03:12,749 --> 00:03:14,247
Not great, on paper.

92
00:03:14,247 --> 00:03:16,058
But look at it subject by subject.

93
00:03:16,058 --> 00:03:18,241
English: ten out of ten.

94
00:03:18,241 --> 00:03:19,367
Full marks.

95
00:03:19,367 --> 00:03:20,737
And Quant…

96
00:03:20,737 --> 00:03:21,619
minus two.

97
00:03:21,619 --> 00:03:22,769
She isn't a weak student.

98
00:03:22,769 --> 00:03:25,358
She's guessing in Maths, and the negative marking

99
00:03:25,358 --> 00:03:27,076
is eating her score.

100
00:03:27,076 --> 00:03:29,677
Topic Performance breaks it down even further,

101
00:03:29,677 --> 00:03:32,950
and Questions breakdown shows every answer she gave,

102
00:03:32,950 --> 00:03:35,284
even the seconds she spent on each one.

103
00:03:35,284 --> 00:03:37,571
It's exactly like opening her copy.

104
00:03:37,571 --> 00:03:39,130
Except you never had to check it.

105
00:03:39,230 --> 00:03:41,540
This list tells you who scored what.

106
00:03:41,540 --> 00:03:42,957
It doesn't tell you why.

107
00:03:42,957 --> 00:03:44,789
For that: Full report.

108
00:03:45,510 --> 00:03:48,169
This is the page to open the morning after.

109
00:03:48,169 --> 00:03:50,734
Forty-eight candidates, the day it was written,

110
00:03:50,734 --> 00:03:52,466
twenty questions, forty marks.

111
00:03:52,566 --> 00:03:54,447
How the class did.

112
00:03:54,447 --> 00:03:58,696
The average: fifty-four percent, about twenty-two

113
00:03:58,696 --> 00:04:00,066
out of forty.

114
00:04:00,066 --> 00:04:02,446
Highest, Aditi, ninety-four.

115
00:04:02,446 --> 00:04:04,234
Lowest, eight.

116
00:04:04,234 --> 00:04:07,032
And eleven candidates need help: they're below forty

117
00:04:07,032 --> 00:04:07,613
percent.

118
00:04:07,713 --> 00:04:09,780
Now this chart, how the marks spread.

119
00:04:09,780 --> 00:04:10,766
Most people skip it.

120
00:04:10,766 --> 00:04:11,869
Please don't.

121
00:04:11,869 --> 00:04:14,667
Each bar is a group of students: zero to nineteen

122
00:04:14,667 --> 00:04:17,849
percent, twenty to thirty-nine, and so on.

123
00:04:17,849 --> 00:04:19,857
Red is trouble, amber is getting there,

124
00:04:19,857 --> 00:04:20,904
green is strong.

125
00:04:21,613 --> 00:04:23,447
And the shape tells you something.

126
00:04:23,447 --> 00:04:26,071
If everything piles up on the right,

127
00:04:26,071 --> 00:04:28,660
the paper was too easy.

128
00:04:28,660 --> 00:04:30,228
Make the next one tougher.

129
00:04:30,228 --> 00:04:31,586
A pile on the left?

130
00:04:31,586 --> 00:04:33,943
Either the paper was too hard, or the chapter didn't

131
00:04:33,943 --> 00:04:34,640
land.

132
00:04:34,640 --> 00:04:37,670
And two humps, one at each end, means your batch has

133
00:04:37,670 --> 00:04:39,330
split into two groups.

134
00:04:39,330 --> 00:04:42,128
That's when a separate doubt class for the lower group

135
00:04:42,128 --> 00:04:43,301
really pays off.

136
00:04:43,301 --> 00:04:46,867
Ours is a healthy middle, with a tail that needs attention.

137
00:04:47,847 --> 00:04:50,694
And next to it, the same story in plain words.

138
00:04:50,794 --> 00:04:54,289
Below that, the rank list, medals for the top three.

139
00:04:54,289 --> 00:04:57,284
Tap Below forty percent, and there are your eleven.

140
00:04:57,284 --> 00:04:58,979
These are the students to call this week,

141
00:04:58,979 --> 00:05:00,767
or whose parents to message.

142
00:05:00,767 --> 00:05:02,833
Search works with roll numbers too,

143
00:05:02,833 --> 00:05:05,251
and any name opens the same answer sheet.

144
00:05:05,351 --> 00:05:08,335
Now, honestly, the most useful card on the whole page:

145
00:05:08,335 --> 00:05:09,972
Teach these again.

146
00:05:09,972 --> 00:05:13,002
Every question fewer than half your batch got right,

147
00:05:13,002 --> 00:05:14,198
hardest first.

148
00:05:14,198 --> 00:05:16,926
So Monday's discussion class doesn't start from question

149
00:05:16,926 --> 00:05:17,669
one.

150
00:05:17,669 --> 00:05:19,294
It starts here.

151
00:05:19,294 --> 00:05:20,813
Five questions, not twenty.

152
00:05:20,913 --> 00:05:22,492
Click Q13.

153
00:05:22,492 --> 00:05:25,348
Two successive discounts, twenty and ten percent.

154
00:05:25,348 --> 00:05:26,660
The answer is twenty-eight.

155
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:29,133
But look: fifty-four percent picked thirty.

156
00:05:29,133 --> 00:05:31,350
They simply added the two discounts.

157
00:05:31,350 --> 00:05:32,465
That's not a silly mistake.

158
00:05:32,465 --> 00:05:34,938
That's a concept the batch has wrong,

159
00:05:34,938 --> 00:05:38,432
and one example on the board fixes it for everyone.

160
00:05:38,532 --> 00:05:40,958
Now Q15, time and work.

161
00:05:40,958 --> 00:05:43,257
Only twenty-one percent got it right.

162
00:05:43,257 --> 00:05:46,427
But look at the grey: almost half left it blank.

163
00:05:46,427 --> 00:05:47,634
They didn't get it wrong.

164
00:05:47,634 --> 00:05:48,575
They never tried it.

165
00:05:48,575 --> 00:05:51,245
So either time ran out, or the chapter isn't done

166
00:05:51,245 --> 00:05:52,000
yet.

167
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,914
Two completely different fixes, and on paper you'd

168
00:05:54,914 --> 00:05:56,472
never have seen the difference.

169
00:05:56,572 --> 00:05:58,488
And Q9, Loktak Lake.

170
00:05:58,488 --> 00:06:00,949
The answers are spread almost evenly across all four

171
00:06:00,949 --> 00:06:01,831
options.

172
00:06:01,831 --> 00:06:03,074
That's what guessing looks like.

173
00:06:03,074 --> 00:06:05,372
Nobody really knew it, so it goes on the revision

174
00:06:05,372 --> 00:06:07,535
list, not into a whole new lesson.

175
00:06:08,251 --> 00:06:11,409
So: one wrong option standing tall is a trap,

176
00:06:11,409 --> 00:06:12,732
reteach the concept.

177
00:06:12,732 --> 00:06:16,552
A lot of grey means time, or an unfinished chapter.

178
00:06:16,552 --> 00:06:18,166
And everything flat?

179
00:06:18,166 --> 00:06:19,071
Guessing.

180
00:06:19,071 --> 00:06:19,915
Revise it.

181
00:06:21,195 --> 00:06:24,678
Flip to Hardest first to see the whole paper by difficulty.

182
00:06:24,678 --> 00:06:27,534
And By section shows your batch subject by subject.

183
00:06:27,534 --> 00:06:28,974
Reasoning, seventy-five.

184
00:06:28,974 --> 00:06:30,077
English, seventy.

185
00:06:30,077 --> 00:06:31,493
General Awareness, fifty-seven.

186
00:06:31,493 --> 00:06:33,014
And Quant, forty-four.

187
00:06:33,014 --> 00:06:35,371
So now you know exactly where next week's extra class

188
00:06:35,371 --> 00:06:35,890
goes.

189
00:06:35,990 --> 00:06:38,103
Finally, sending it out.

190
00:06:38,103 --> 00:06:40,715
Send rank list opens WhatsApp with the whole list

191
00:06:40,715 --> 00:06:43,722
already typed, and you choose who gets it.

192
00:06:43,722 --> 00:06:46,149
Copy, if you'd rather paste it somewhere else.

193
00:06:46,149 --> 00:06:48,958
Excel gives you two sheets: the rank list,

194
00:06:48,958 --> 00:06:51,675
and every question with its most-picked wrong option.

195
00:06:51,675 --> 00:06:54,730
And Print, for your file or the parents' meeting.

196
00:06:55,461 --> 00:06:57,690
One last place: All Submissions.

197
00:06:57,690 --> 00:07:00,697
Every exam your candidates wrote, newest first.

198
00:07:00,697 --> 00:07:03,751
The badge is the class average, and the coloured bar

199
00:07:03,751 --> 00:07:05,515
is strong, average and weak.

200
00:07:05,515 --> 00:07:07,884
Now read it from the bottom up: forty-six,

201
00:07:07,884 --> 00:07:09,823
forty-eight, fifty, fifty-four.

202
00:07:09,823 --> 00:07:11,854
Week by week, your batch is climbing,

203
00:07:11,854 --> 00:07:14,420
and that's something you can actually show a parent.

204
00:07:14,420 --> 00:07:16,591
Next week's mock is waiting right there,

205
00:07:16,591 --> 00:07:18,054
one tap from going live.

206
00:07:18,765 --> 00:07:20,599
So here's your five-minute routine,

207
00:07:20,599 --> 00:07:22,120
every Monday morning.

208
00:07:22,120 --> 00:07:24,674
One: look at the average and the spread.

209
00:07:24,674 --> 00:07:26,671
Two: send the rank list.

210
00:07:26,671 --> 00:07:29,388
Three: open Teach these again.

211
00:07:29,388 --> 00:07:31,327
That's your discussion class.

212
00:07:31,327 --> 00:07:35,460
Four: filter below forty percent, and call those students.

213
00:07:35,460 --> 00:07:37,840
Five: download the Excel for your register.

214
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:38,421
Five minutes.

215
00:07:38,421 --> 00:07:39,510
Not a whole Sunday.

216
00:07:40,760 --> 00:07:42,409
Paper tests gave you marks.

217
00:07:42,409 --> 00:07:44,963
TestoZa gives you the reasons behind them.

218
00:07:44,963 --> 00:07:47,494
Try it on your next mock, and if this helped,

219
00:07:47,494 --> 00:07:49,526
you know where the subscribe button is.

220
00:07:49,526 --> 00:07:50,697
See you in the next one.
